<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Random Student Picker | Classroom Name Selector</title><meta name="description" content="Paste a class list and randomly select students one at a time. Optional remove-after-pick mode and selection history. Free classroom tool."><meta name="robots" content="index,follow"><link rel="canonical" href="https://wheelnamepicker.com.au/student-picker"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/value-pages.css"></head><body><div class="top"><div class="nav"><a class="brand" href="/">Wheel Name Picker</a><div class="links"><a href="/team-picker">Team Picker</a><a href="/weekday-picker">Weekday Picker</a><a href="/guides/classroom-fair-picking">Classroom Guide</a></div></div></div><header class="hero"><span class="badge">Classroom tool</span><h1>Random Student Picker</h1><p>Paste one student name per line, then select a name at random. Keep everyone in the pool for independent draws or remove names after selection so every student is chosen once before the list resets.</p></header><main class="wrap"><div class="tool"><div class="grid"><div class="field"><label for="names">Student names</label><textarea id="names">Alex
Bailey
Charlie
Dakota
Elliot
Frankie</textarea></div><div class="field"><label for="mode">Selection mode</label><select id="mode"><option value="keep">Keep selected names in the list</option><option value="remove">Remove after selection</option></select><label>Selection history</label><div class="result" id="history">No selections yet.</div></div></div><div class="actions"><button id="pick">Pick a student</button><button class="secondary" id="reset">Clear history</button></div><div class="result big-choice" id="result">Ready</div></div><div class="article"><section><h2>Why use a random student picker?</h2><p>A visible random process can help teachers vary participation instead of repeatedly calling on the same volunteers. It can be useful for warm-up questions, reading turns, group demonstrations, classroom jobs and low-stakes review activities.</p><p>Random selection should still be used with professional judgement. A student may need an alternative participation method, extra processing time or an exemption from public answering. The tool supports classroom organisation; it does not replace inclusive teaching decisions.</p></section><section><h2>Keep names or remove them?</h2><p><strong>Keep selected names</strong> means every pick starts from the full list again. This is appropriate when repeated selection is acceptable. <strong>Remove after selection</strong> means a selected name is deleted from the text box, so each remaining student has a chance before the pool is exhausted.</p><p>The selection history stays on the page until you clear it, which makes it easier to see who has already been picked during the current activity.</p></section><section><h2>Practical classroom uses</h2><ul><li>Choose a student to answer a review question after giving the whole class thinking time.</li><li>Select a helper for distributing materials or collecting books.</li><li>Pick the first presenter when several groups are ready.</li><li>Rotate discussion starters without creating a fixed order.</li><li>Choose which table or group packs away first.</li></ul></section><section><h2>Privacy</h2><p>The names entered here are processed in the browser. This page does not require an account and does not intentionally upload the class list to a server. On a shared device, clear the names when you finish if you do not want them left on screen.</p></section><section><h2>Make random selection feel fair</h2><p>Tell students how the picker will be used before the activity starts. For assessed work or sensitive topics, use a process that matches your school's policies rather than relying on an informal random picker. For everyday participation, a clear rule such as “everyone gets think time, then one name is selected” can make the process more predictable and less stressful.</p><p><a href="/guides/classroom-fair-picking">Read the classroom fair-picking guide →</a></p></section></div></main><footer class="footer"><a href="/about">About</a><a href="/privacy">Privacy</a><a href="/terms">Terms</a><a href="/contact/">Contact</a><p>© 2026 Wheel Name Picker · Ascension Digital Group</p></footer><script>const $=id=>document.getElementById(id);let log=[];function list(){return $('names').value.split(/\n|,/).map(x=>x.trim()).filter(Boolean)}$('pick').onclick=()=>{const arr=list();if(!arr.length)return $('result').textContent='Add at least one name.';const i=Math.floor(Math.random()*arr.length),name=arr[i];$('result').textContent=name;log.unshift(name);$('history').innerHTML=log.map((x,i)=>`${i+1}. ${x}`).join('<br>');if($('mode').value==='remove'){arr.splice(i,1);$('names').value=arr.join('\n')}};$('reset').onclick=()=>{log=[];$('history').textContent='No selections yet.';$('result').textContent='Ready'};</script></body></html>